 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 334270,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/f91946e9b6eb4acb.jpg\" alt=\"This table has three headers: Organism, Temperature in degrees Celsius and Average respiration in ML O2 per gram per minute. At a temperature of 10 degrees, the first mouse has an average respiration of 0.0518. At a temperature of 25 degrees, the second mouse has an average respiration of 0.0321. At a temperature of 10 degrees, the first cricket has an average respiration of 0.0013. At a temperature of 25 degrees, the second cricket has an average respiration of 0.0038.\"></p>\n<p>Evaluate the table pictured. According to the data, the crickets at 25°C have greater oxygen consumption per gram of tissue than do the crickets at 10°C. This trend in oxygen consumption is the opposite of that in mice. Make a claim to explain the reason for this difference in trends in oxygen consumption among crickets and mice.</p>\n",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 842740,
     "content_html": "It is caused by the animals' difference in size."
    },
    {
     "id": 842741,
     "content_html": "It is a result of the animals' different modes of nutrition."
    },
    {
     "id": 842743,
     "content_html": "It is a result of the animals' differences in metabolic heat production."
    },
    {
     "id": 842742,
     "content_html": "It is a reflection of the animals' different modes of ATP production."
    }
   ],
